concepts/i18n.content:
Internationalization (i18n)

This project supports multiple languages using react-i18next. Currently supported languages are English (default) and Spanish.
Architecture
Configuration
The i18n configuration is in src/Infrastructure/i18n.ts:
import i18n from &#039;i18next&#039;;
import { initReactI18next } from &#039;react-i18next&#039;;
import LanguageDetector from &#039;i18next-browser-languagedetector&#039;;

// Import translation files
import enCommon from &#039;@shared/Locales/en/common.json&#039;;
// ... other namespaces

i18n
  .use(LanguageDetector)
  .use(initReactI18next)
  .init({
    resources: { en: {...}, es: {...} },
    fallbackLng: &#039;en&#039;,
    supportedLngs: [&#039;en&#039;, &#039;es&#039;],
    // ...
  });
Translation Files
Translations are organized by language and namespace:
src/Shared/Locales/
├── en/
│   ├── common.json      # Common UI strings (buttons, labels, themes)
│   ├── apps.json        # App names, descriptions, and folder names (folders.games, folders.office, etc.)
│   ├── menuedit.json    # MenuEditApp strings (predefined, customFolders, availableApps, etc.)
│   ├── settings.json    # Settings sections and options
│   ├── window.json      # Window-related strings
│   ├── contextMenu.json # Context menu strings
│   ├── notifications.json # Notification messages
│   ├── welcome.json       # Welcome app strings
└── es/
    └── ...              # Same structure

Use the useTranslation Hook
import { useTranslation } from &#039;react-i18next&#039;;

const MyComponent = () =&gt; {
  const { t, i18n } = useTranslation();

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{t(&#039;common:labels.title&#039;)}&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Current language: {i18n.language}&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; i18n.changeLanguage(&#039;es&#039;)}&gt;
        Switch to Spanish
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};
Language Detection

First visit: The app detects the browser’s language using navigator.language. If it’s a supported language (en/es), it’s used. Otherwise, English is the default.
Fallback: If the detected language is not supported, English is used
Persistence: User’s language preference is saved to localStorage with key fde-desktop:language (used by i18next-browser-languagedetector)
Manual switch: Users can change language via:

Settings app → Language section
Taskbar language selector (flag button)



Language Synchronization
The useLanguageSync hook ensures the Zustand store and i18next stay synchronized:
import { useLanguageSync } from &#039;@presentation/Hooks/useLanguageSync&#039;;

// In your root component (App.tsx)
function App() {
  useLanguageSync(); // Keeps store and i18n in sync
  // ...
}
How it works:

On mount, it checks if the store’s language differs from i18next’s current language
If they differ, it calls i18n.changeLanguage() to sync them
It also saves the language to localStorage under fde-desktop:language for persistence across sessions

Location: packages/core/src/hooks/useLanguageSync.ts
Adding a New Language


Create a new folder in packages/core/src/i18n/locales/ (e.g., fr/)


Copy all JSON files from en/ to the new folder


Translate all strings in the JSON files


Update packages/core/src/i18n/index.ts:
import frCommon from &#039;./locales/fr/common.json&#039;;
// ... import other namespaces

export const SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES = [&#039;en&#039;, &#039;es&#039;, &#039;fr&#039;] as const;

// Add to resources:
fr: {
  common: frCommon,
  // ...
}


Update LanguageSettings.tsx and LanguageSelector.tsx with the new language flag and name


Translation Keys Convention
Use nested keys organized by feature:
{
  &quot;folders&quot;: {
    &quot;games&quot;: &quot;Games&quot;,
    &quot;office&quot;: &quot;Office and media&quot;,
    &quot;programming&quot;: &quot;Programming&quot;,
    &quot;socialLinks&quot;: &quot;Social Links&quot;
  },
  &quot;actions&quot;: {
    &quot;save&quot;: &quot;Save&quot;,
    &quot;cancel&quot;: &quot;Cancel&quot;
  },
  &quot;labels&quot;: {
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Name&quot;,
    &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Title&quot;
  },
  &quot;messages&quot;: {
    &quot;areYouSure&quot;: &quot;Are you sure?&quot;,
    &quot;cannotBeUndone&quot;: &quot;This action cannot be undone.&quot;
  }
}
Access them with: t(&#039;namespace:key.path&#039;) or t(&#039;key.path&#039;) if using default namespace.
Interpolation
Use double curly braces for dynamic values:
{
  &quot;messages&quot;: {
    &quot;replaceFileMessage&quot;: &quot;A file named \&quot;{{name}}\&quot; already exists.&quot;
  }
}
t(&#039;messages.replaceFileMessage&#039;, { name: &#039;document.txt&#039; });
Testing with i18n
The test setup includes mocks for both react-i18next and i18next in src/Shared/Testing/__mocks__/jsdom-setup.tsx. The mock returns translation keys as values with namespace and interpolation support:
// The mock handles namespaces and interpolation
vi.mock(&#039;react-i18next&#039;, () =&gt; ({
  useTranslation: (defaultNs?: string) =&gt; ({
    t: (key: string, options?: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;) =&gt; {
      if (options?.defaultValue) return options.defaultValue as string;
      const ns = (options?.ns as string | undefined) ?? defaultNs;
      let result = key;
      if (ns &amp;&amp; !result.includes(&#039;:&#039;)) {
        result = `${ns}:${result}`;
      }
      // Interpolation: {{name}} -&gt; value
      if (options) {
        Object.entries(options).forEach(([k, v]) =&gt; {
          if (k !== &#039;ns&#039; &amp;&amp; k !== &#039;defaultValue&#039;) {
            result = result.replace(new RegExp(`{{\\s*${k}\\s*}}`, &#039;g&#039;), String(v));
          }
        });
      }
      return result;
    },
    i18n: { changeLanguage: vi.fn(), language: &#039;en&#039; },
  }),
  Trans: ({ children }) =&gt; children,
  initReactI18next: { type: &#039;3rdParty&#039;, init: vi.fn() },
}));
Tests should use translation keys instead of hardcoded strings:
// Good: using translation key
expect(screen.getByLabelText(&#039;window.ariaLabels.close&#039;)).toBeInTheDocument();

// Good: using role-based queries with translation keys
expect(screen.getByRole(&#039;button&#039;, { name: &#039;common:actions.save&#039; })).toBeInTheDocument();

// Good: with interpolation
expect(screen.getByText(&#039;messages.replaceFileMessage: {{name}}test.txt&#039;)).toBeInTheDocument();
For files that import i18next directly (e.g., menu bar builders), the mock is also provided:
vi.mock(&#039;i18next&#039;, () =&gt; ({
  default: {
    t: (key: string) =&gt; key,
    use: vi.fn(() =&gt; mockI18n),
    init: vi.fn(() =&gt; mockI18n),
    changeLanguage: vi.fn(),
    language: &#039;en&#039;,
  },
}));
UI Components for Language Selection
LanguageSettings
Located in src/Presentation/Components/Apps/SettingsApp/sections/LanguageSettings.tsx:

Shows list of available languages with flags
Highlights currently selected language
Persists selection to settingsStore

LanguageSelector
Located in src/Presentation/Components/Taskbar/LanguageSelector.tsx:

Compact button showing current language flag
Popover menu for quick language switching
Integrated into the taskbar system tray

